course: dictionary
lesson: 1399
title: Caraway, a spice that is really a fruit
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: Some words have two meanings, and context tells you which one is meant. In «Some molecules come in mirror-image pairs, like left and right gloves, and the two can smell different: one version of carvone smells of spearmint, its mirror twin of caraway seeds.», the clue is the smell. Caraway here names a taste and smell, the warm, liquorice-like flavour of the seeds.
explain: Check the other sense too. Caraway can also name the plant, which is related to the carrot. Ask yourself: is the sentence about a plant growing, or about something you can taste and smell? If it is about tasting and smelling, the word means the spice.
explain: Caraway is an uncountable noun, so you say «caraway» for the spice, not «three caraways». You can say «caraway seeds» when you mean the small brown seeds. Try swapping in your meaning: does «the seeds smell of caraway» still make sense? If yes, you have it right.
fact: Caraway is a plant related to the carrot.
fact: Caraway means the small, curved, brown seeds used as a spice, with a warm, slightly liquorice-like taste.
fact: Caraway seeds go in rye bread, seed cake, sauerkraut and some cheeses.
fact: In science, caraway seeds are actually small dry fruits.
fact: Caraway is uncountable, so we do not say «two caraways» for the spice.
exercise: In a recipe for rye bread, what is caraway? | * A spice made from small, curved, brown seeds | A large green leaf for wrapping food | A kind of sweet, sticky sauce | A type of flour
exercise: «one version of carvone smells of spearmint, its mirror twin of caraway seeds.» What does the mirror twin smell of? | * Caraway seeds | Spearmint | Lemons | Fresh rain
exercise: In science, what are caraway seeds really? | Stones from a hard rock | Roots from under the ground | * Small dry fruits | Tiny green leaves
test: Caraway is related to which vegetable? | Onion | * Carrot | Cabbage | Potato
test: What does caraway seed taste like? | Sweet, like honey | * Warm and slightly like liquorice | Sour, like a lemon | Salty, like crisps
exam: Which sentence uses caraway correctly for the spice? | * Mum sprinkled caraway on the seed cake. | The caraways of the garden were tall. | Mum counted three caraways in the spice jar. | Mum caraways the seed cake.
